# Accessibility priorities

The core product — dance tutorial videos — is inherently visual, and that's fine.
Don't invest in things that try to make the *demonstration itself* non-visual
(e.g. full audio-description tracks narrating every movement). That's genuinely
low ROI for this product.

But most accessibility work isn't about the video content — it's about the
generic site shell around it, and that part is high-value regardless of how
visual the product is:

## Always do (cheap, high-value, no excuse to skip)

- Forms, checkout, navigation, buttons, focus states, ARIA semantics — these
  are the same generic UI patterns any site has. A screen-reader or
  keyboard-only user still needs to sign up, browse the catalog, and pay.
  Missing focus indicators, unlabeled buttons, wrong ARIA roles, etc. are just
  bugs, not "accessibility we chose to skip."
- These are near-free to fix (usually a few lines per instance) — treat them
  like any other bug, not an optional investment.

## Deprioritize until there's real demand/budget

- Real video captions/subtitles (actual VTT content authoring, not just the
  `<track>` wiring) is an ongoing content-production cost, not a one-time code
  fix. Fair to deprioritize until there's real user demand or content budget
  for it — don't build this out speculatively.
- Note captions also have a non-accessibility business case (dancers watching
  with sound off, or wanting counts as text) — worth revisiting if that
  demand shows up, independent of accessibility framing.

## Also worth remembering

- Legal exposure (ADA/WCAG-style claims) targets exactly the generic-UI gaps
  above, regardless of how visual the core product is — another reason not to
  skip the cheap stuff.
